WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.933
Last clip your instance
went behind its own proxy.

00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:05.533
Now keep it current without
risking what's inside.

00:00:05.533 --> 00:00:08.933
Upgrade without losing a thing,
and know exactly what happened.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.600
By the end you'll upgrade in the right
order, know what gets checked first,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.567
and know what happens if it fails.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.133
Leo upgrades late one evening,
a migration fails halfway,

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:21.467
and nobody knows which version is
running or what state the data is in.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.200
These steps exist so
that story never happens.

00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:25.833
First, who runs what.

00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:30.300
Before this release, the public
images were only 0.2.2 and stable.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.333
CLI versions 0.2.3 and
0.2.4 shipped no images.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:37.533
If you installed either, your
instance still runs the 0.2.2 images.

00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:41.433
The CLI version and the instance
version are separate things.

00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:46.867
In the console: Settings, Updates, for
instance admins. Or ask your agent:

00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:50.933
"Check whether an upgrade is
available and what it would change".

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.167
Then the CLI, always first.
Install it globally at latest.

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:57.067
The CLI renders the compose
file your instance runs on.

00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.900
So it must be at least as new
as the release you're moving to.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.333
If it's too old, gtm upgrade stops and
tells you which version to install.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:06.133
Second, look before you leap.

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.000
Run gtm upgrade with check,
which shows both versions.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.833
Then run gtm upgrade with dry
run from the instance folder.

00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:16.133
It prints every command the upgrade
would run. Both change nothing.

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:20.300
One habit worth copying: run the
dry run every time, and read it.

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:23.200
Third, the real run: gtm upgrade with yes.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.500
Yes is required, because it
migrates your live database.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.900
Before anything changes,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.400
the signed release list is
fetched and its signature checked.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.100
The path is planned by rules: no
downgrade, no skipped required stop,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.367
additive only, and a minimum CLI.

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:42.133
Then the images are pulled, and each
digest must match the signed one.

00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:46.200
Every pending database change is read,
and anything that drops, renames,

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.500
retypes, deletes or overwrites is refused.

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:52.167
Now the order that matters:
backup, rehearsal, migrate.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:55.867
The services stop gracefully, and
a backup is taken and verified.

00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:57.733
A rehearsal restores that backup into

00:01:57.733 --> 00:01:59.833
a scratch database and migrates it there.

00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:01.933
Then it compares tables, columns,

00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:04.300
row counts and checksums
with your live data.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.200
Any difference refuses the upgrade.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.367
Only then does the real
migration run, followed by start,

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:13.200
health check and verification. A
flag to skip the rehearsal exists.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.233
Don't use it.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:15.500
Fourth, what's kept.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.833
All of it: records, history, definitions,
approvals, connections and keys.

00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:24.200
Nothing is dropped, recreated or
rewritten. Your data stays yours.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.800
Your master key isn't in any
backup, so keep it somewhere safe,

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.200
separate from the dump.

00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:33.833
If an upgrade fails part way, it
rolls back by swapping images.

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:37.500
The previous compose and env files
and the previous images come back,

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.267
and the services restart on them.

00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:43.167
The database is never restored,
dropped or recreated by an upgrade.

00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:45.900
Restoring a backup is
a manual last resort,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.700
for damage something else caused.
Never restore over the live database.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.467
Rename it first, then
restore into a new one.

00:02:52.467 --> 00:02:55.333
gtm upgrade with help
prints the exact commands.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.967
The worry: a rollback that never
restores the database sounds risky.

00:02:58.967 --> 00:03:01.733
It's the safe part. Every
migration only adds,

00:03:01.733 --> 00:03:04.867
so the previous code runs
fine on the migrated database.

00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:07.700
Nothing was dropped, so
nothing needs restoring.

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:09.733
Fifth, two things to expect.

00:03:09.733 --> 00:03:13.500
Coming from the older images,
expect local only ports.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.400
The console, API and MCP
bind to 127.0.0.1 by default.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.467
Only your machine reaches them. Both
gtm up and gtm upgrade warn you first,

00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:27.367
and the setting lands in your .env.
If people reach the server by address,

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:30.633
set GTMOS_BIND_ADDRESS
or run gtm up with bind.

00:03:30.633 --> 00:03:33.767
Better still, put a
reverse proxy in front.

00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:36.933
Then the apps. An upgrade
changes no installed engine app.

00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:41.267
New versions arrive through apps
update, which is owner or admin only.

00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:45.367
In the console: Settings, Packages,
Engine app updates, Propose update.

00:03:45.367 --> 00:03:47.900
Or ask your agent: "Which
engine apps have updates".

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.733
A second person approves,

00:03:49.733 --> 00:03:52.633
and your edits are merged
in with a three way merge.

00:03:52.633 --> 00:03:54.433
If an edit overlaps the release,

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:57.433
that app waits until you
choose Keep mine or Take new.

00:03:57.467 --> 00:04:01.367
Last, work the checklist. The doctor
shows the sources you expect as live,

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:03.533
and its Harness section has no stale line.

00:04:03.533 --> 00:04:07.833
Health reports the new version on
the API, worker, MCP and console.

00:04:07.833 --> 00:04:11.100
The upgrade said verification
passed, a snapshot exists,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.467
and your row counts match.

00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:16.600
Preview the rescan before the first
one, because scores can move once.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.867
Propose one send to an internal address,

00:04:18.867 --> 00:04:21.233
and confirm a different
person must approve it.

00:04:21.233 --> 00:04:25.233
Then run gtm init with refresh in
each project. Or ask your agent:

00:04:25.233 --> 00:04:28.567
"Run the post upgrade checklist
and tell me what is not green".

00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:33.900
Run it back: CLI first, then check and
dry run. Backup, rehearsal, migrate.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.933
An upgrade only adds, and a
failure swaps images back,

00:04:36.933 --> 00:04:38.733
never touching your data.

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:41.267
You get a signed path,
a rehearsal on a copy,

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:43.367
and a rollback that
never touches your data.

00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:45.967
Keep your master key somewhere separate.

00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:48.633
Coming next, Tutorial:
when something looks wrong,

00:04:48.633 --> 00:04:50.833
where the cheapest check comes first.
